Now I understand the vulnerability. The `geo.lat` and `geo.lon` buffers are only 16 bytes (`ICAL_GEO_LEN = 16`). In `simple_str_to_doublestr`, the check is:

```c
if (end - start + 1 > 100) {
    return 1;
}
```

This allows up to 100 characters to be copied. But the copy loop is:

```c
for (i = 0; i < end - from; ++i) {
    if (start[i] == '.' && ...) {
        result[i] = loc_data->decimal_point[0];
    } else {
        result[i] = start[i];
    }
}
```

Wait, let me re-examine. The loop condition is `i < end - from`, but it writes to `result[i]` using `start[i]`. The `end - from` could be larger than `end - start` if there are leading whitespaces. But more importantly, the check `end - start + 1 > 100` limits the number of digits, but the copy loop writes `end - from` characters to `result`, which is a 16-byte buffer. So if there are leading whitespaces, `end - from` can be much larger than `end - start`.

